  for you to review, then an implementation plan. Part 2 is short.

  [1mDesign, part 2 of 2: error handling and testing[0m

  [1mError handling:[0m
  - [1mlocalStorage is unavailable or throws[0m (private mode, storage blocked or full). [38;5;153mSession[39m catches the error.
    [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m and [38;5;153msetUserId()[39m does nothing. The user just sees the [38;5;153muserId[39m field every time, and login
    still works.
  - [1mMissing [38;5;153muserId[0m: [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m returns [38;5;153m{ valid: false, error: "Missing required fields" }[39m, the same message as for a
    missing username or password.
  - [1mWhitespace-only IDs[0m count as missing. IDs are trimmed before they're validated and saved.
  - [1mFailed login[0m: the ID is saved only after [38;5;153mlogin[39m succeeds, so a mistyped ID from a failed attempt isn't kept.
  - [1m[38;5;153msession.js[39m loaded after [38;5;153mapp.js[39m by mistake[0m: the submit handler would throw a [38;5;153mReferenceError[39m when it touches [38;5;153mSession[39m.
    I'll keep the load-order requirement in a comment at the top of [38;5;153msession.js[39m rather than add defensive code.

  [1mTesting:[0m
  - The repo has no test runner. I'd use Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m (no new dependencies) and add [38;5;153m"test": "node --test"[39m
    to [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m.
  - So that Node can load [38;5;153msession.js[39m and the logic in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, each file ends with a guarded [38;5;153mif (typeof module !== [39m
    [38;5;153m"undefined") module.exports = ...[39m. The browser ignores that line. The DOM wiring in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m would also need a guard
    so it doesn't run under Node.
  - Unit tests:
    - [38;5;153mSession[39m get/set/clear against a fake localStorage, including the case where storage throws
    - [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m with and without [38;5;153muserId[39m
    - [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns the [38;5;153muserId[39m and never logs the password
  - Manual check in the browser:
    - First visit shows the field
    - After login, a reload hides the field and shows "Logged in as…"
    - "Not you?" brings the field back

  Does part 2 look right? If it does, I'll write everything into
  [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-10-03-userid-login-design.md[39m (not committed) for you to review, then turn it into an
  implementation plan.

